  "enhanced_information": "The 65-year-old man exhibits inappropriate behavior, ataxic gait, and amnesia, suggesting a neurodegenerative disorder. The negative urine drug screen indicates that acute intoxication is unlikely. Among the options, Lewy bodies are associated with Lewy body dementia, which can present with cognitive decline, behavioral changes, and motor symptoms like ataxia. Pick bodies are linked to frontotemporal dementia, which typically presents with personality changes and language difficulties rather than ataxia. PrPSC sheets are associated with prion diseases, which are less common and would typically present with different symptoms. Amyloid plaques are characteristic of Alzheimer's disease, which primarily affects memory but does not typically present with ataxia. Therefore, the most likely pathological finding in this patient is Lewy bodies, as they align with the observed symptoms and the consensus on neurodegenerative disorders.",
